Chapter: QuoteFromPeer

  cites Foreword chapter Console
  quotes "sha256:71f85b21050759c45724ab3a7eebe9eb36453b16b5c3878c6fdea78732bce71b" as Sorted
  trusting "fcfbf1f0f3f0f3ff" "30a23f41fc17ec2aac012d0755750484a535afbcff80882094d569bbc7bdefff" 10000
  trusting above 5000

 The same quotation a third way. codex/test/quotes-gate.codex proves it
 through the offered-works blob, codex/test/quote-from-store.codex proves
 it through the fact store on an attached disk, and this proves it
 through a peer: the work is on another machine's disk, and the only
 thing this source has is the digest.

 There is no %%QUOTED-WORKS%% section here and no store attached. The
 host asks a peer for the work by that digest, and prepends what comes
 back to the blob before the compiler ever sees the source. The compiler
 is not told where the work came from and has no way to ask -- which is
 the point. It hashes the content, checks the signature, checks the key
 this source pinned, and checks the floor, exactly as it does for a work
 that arrived beside the source or off the local disk. A peer that lies
 is refused by arithmetic; a peer that is honest but unknown is refused
 by the floor. That is what lets the messenger be untrusted, and it is
 why peer resolution can live in the host rather than in the compiler.

 The trusted key is named here, in the source, and never by the peer. The
 works arrive over a transport, and a transport must not be able to vouch
 for its own cargo.

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni ("sorted=" & show (sort-ascending 5))
  end

